---
// Privacy Policy — PLACEHOLDER. See terms.astro for why this page exists.
//
// 2026-08-10: the in-person disaster check was removed from the product, so the
// clause promising that a recipient's details would be passed to the Cajun Navy
// Ground Force for a door knock is gone. It is replaced by an explicit denial
// rather than silence — this page previously told people their parent's address
// would be handed to a third party, and anyone who read it deserves to see that
// withdrawn in as many words. The ZIP code's stated purpose is now the true one:
// it sets the recipient's time zone so the call lands at the right hour.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LegalDraft from '../components/LegalDraft.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy Policy · Aunt Mabel" noindex={true}>
  <LegalDraft heading="Privacy Policy" kicker="Legal">
    <p>
      This policy will describe how <strong>Trustlight LLC</strong>, the company
      that operates the Aunt Mabel daily wellness-call service, handles the
      information collected through it.
    </p>
    <p class="emphasis">
      We do not share a recipient's details with the Cajun Navy Ground Force.
      An earlier version of this page said we passed their address on for an
      in-person wellness check after a disaster; that check is not part of the
      service, and no such sharing takes place.
    </p>
    <p>
      This policy is being finalized with counsel now. When it's ready, this page
      will set out what we collect, why we collect it, how long we keep it, who
      it is shared with, and how to ask for it to be deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things are worth stating up front, because they're the ones families
      ask about first:
    </p>
    <p class="emphasis">
      Daily calls are not recorded today. Preserving call recordings for
      families is a possible future feature, and it would require its own
      separate, explicit consent — it would never be switched on quietly for
      anyone already enrolled.
    </p>
    <p>
      The details a recipient shares on a call are used to check on their
      wellbeing and, if something sounds wrong, to alert the account holder and
      the emergency contacts named during enrollment. Those alerts are sent by
      email. A recipient's ZIP code is used to work out their time zone, so the
      daily call arrives at the right hour where they actually are.
    </p>
  </LegalDraft>
</BaseLayout>
